Package: openastro-guider
Architecture: arm64
# Versioned against the old file-shipping packages only, so the empty
# openastro-phd2/phd2-alpaca transitionals (built at this same version, which
# Depend on openastro-guider) can coexist and do the migration. An unversioned
# Conflicts would make those transitionals uninstallable.
Breaks: openastro-phd2 (<< 2.0.0), phd2-alpaca (<< 2.0.0)
Replaces: openastro-phd2 (<< 2.0.0), phd2-alpaca (<< 2.0.0)
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
         adduser,
         xvfb
Description: OpenAstro headless Alpaca-only auto-guiding engine
 OpenAstro Guider is a Linux-only, headless guiding engine derived from PHD2.
 Connect your mount, your camera, select a star, and start guiding — driven
 remotely over PHD2's JSON-RPC event-server API.
 .
 This build reaches the guide camera, mount, and rotator over ASCOM Alpaca
 on the local network.
 .
 Runs headlessly as the openastro-guider system user via systemd, exposing the
 PHD2 JSON-RPC API for remote control by planetarium and capture software
 (primarily OpenAstro Ara).

Package: openastro-phd2
Architecture: all
Section: oldlibs
Priority: optional
Depends: openastro-guider (= ${binary:Version}), ${misc:Depends}
Description: transitional package - renamed to openastro-guider
 The headless guiding engine was renamed from openastro-phd2 to
 openastro-guider to match the open-astro/openastro-guider repository and to
 distinguish it from the openastro-phd2 GUI/INDI reference build.
 .
 This is an empty transitional metadata package that depends on
 openastro-guider so apt-managed upgrades from a host that had openastro-phd2
 installed pull in openastro-guider automatically. Once the upgrade completes
 you can safely remove this package with `sudo apt remove openastro-phd2` -
 no service or files are owned by it.

Package: phd2-alpaca
Architecture: all
Section: oldlibs
Priority: optional
Depends: openastro-guider (= ${binary:Version}), ${misc:Depends}
Description: transitional package - renamed to openastro-guider
 The guiding engine package was previously named phd2-alpaca, then
 openastro-phd2, and is now openastro-guider.
 .
 This is an empty transitional metadata package that depends on
 openastro-guider. It exists so that apt-managed upgrades from a host that
 had phd2-alpaca installed pull in openastro-guider automatically. Once the
 upgrade completes you can safely remove this package with
 `sudo apt remove phd2-alpaca` - no service or files are owned by it.
